The necessary conditions for a bloodborne pathogen infection to occur are direct contact with infected blood or body fluids, access to the body via an entry site (such as broken skin, mucous membranes, or the respiratory system), and the pathogen must be present in the individual's blood or body fluids. Universal precautions and personal protective equipment (PPE) are critical to preventing transmission. Bloodborne pathogens cannot be spread through casual contact, water, or food.
